    "body": "### Contact Details (e.g. Tox ID or email)\n\n_No response_\n\n### Feature Description\n\n### Description\n\n### Quick summary\n\nCurrently, the window size and state are stored in binary data under `MainWindowGeometry`, and `MainWindowState`. While this works, it makes it difficult for humans to interpret or script modifications to the configuration file. This means that maximizing the window can only be reproduced by copying in the correct blob of binary data.\n\n#### Problem\n- Settings like `MainWindowGeometry` contain complex Qt `ByteArray` data that are not human-readable.  \n- Users who want to configure the application via config files cannot easily set the window to maximized without going through trial and error or copying data from another setup.  \n- The settings file lacks clarity and overview for anyone editing it directly.\n\n#### Proposed Solution\nIntroduce a simple boolean in the settings file, such as:\n\n`window_maximized=false # default`\n\n\n### Reason\n\n- Easier scripting and automation of configuration.  \n- Better readability for users modifying settings files.  \n- Resolution-independent way of ensuring the application always starts maximized.  \n\n\n### Examples\n\nIf set to `true`, the window should open maximized regardless of `MainWindowGeometry` or screen resolution. When `false`, the application should fall back to using the saved `MainWindowGeometry` values.  \n\nThis will make it convenient to use a Bash script to apply maximized window with:\n```\n#!/bin/bash\n\ntee $HOME/.config/tox/qtox.ini << 'EOF'\n[State]\nwindow_maximized=true\nEOF\n```\n\n### Contribution\n\n- [ ] I would like to contribute to this feature",
